#. type: Title =
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "Large Object Promisors"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:7
msgid ""
"Since Git has been created, users have been complaining about issues with "
"storing large files in Git. Some solutions have been created to help, but "
"they haven't helped much with some issues."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:11
msgid ""
"Git currently supports multiple promisor remotes, which could help with some "
"of these remaining issues, but it's very hard to use them to help, because a "
"number of important features are missing."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:14
msgid ""
"The goal of the effort described in this document is to add these important "
"features."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:19
msgid ""
"We will call a \"Large Object Promisor\", or \"LOP\" in short, a promisor "
"remote which is used to store only large blobs and which is separate from "
"the main remote that should store the other Git objects and the rest of the "
"repos."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:23
msgid ""
"By extension, we will also call \"Large Object Promisor\", or LOP, the "
"effort described in this document to add a set of features to make it easier "
"to handle large blobs/files in Git by using LOPs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:27
msgid ""
"This effort aims to especially improve things on the server side, and "
"especially for large blobs that are already compressed in a binary format."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:34
msgid ""
"This effort aims to provide an alternative to Git LFS (https://git-lfs.com/) "
"and similar tools like git-annex (https://git-annex.branchable.com/) for "
"handling large files, even though a complete alternative would very likely "
"require other efforts especially on the client side, where it would likely "
"help to implement a new object representation for large blobs as discussed "
"in:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:36
msgid "https://lore.kernel.org/git/xmqqbkdometi.fsf@gitster.g/"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:38
#, no-wrap
msgid "Non goals"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:42
msgid ""
"We will not discuss those client side improvements here, as they would "
"require changes in different parts of Git than this effort."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:47
msgid ""
"So we don't pretend to fully replace Git LFS with only this effort, but we "
"nevertheless believe that it can significantly improve the current situation "
"on the server side, and that other separate efforts could also improve the "
"situation on the client side."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:51
msgid ""
"In the same way, we are not going to discuss all the possible ways to "
"implement a LOP or their underlying object storage, or to optimize how LOP "
"works."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:60
msgid ""
"Our opinion is that the simplest solution for now is for LOPs to use object "
"storage through a remote helper (see section II.2 below for more details) to "
"store their objects. So we consider that this is the default "
"implementation. If there are improvements on top of this, that's great, but "
"our opinion is that such improvements are not necessary for LOPs to already "
"be useful. Such improvements are likely a different technical topic, and can "
"be taken care of separately anyway."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:67
msgid ""
"So in particular we are not going to discuss pluggable ODBs or other object "
"database backends that could chunk large blobs, dedup the chunks and store "
"them efficiently. Sure, that would be a nice improvement to store large "
"blobs on the server side, but we believe it can just be a separate effort as "
"it's also not technically very related to this effort."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:73
msgid ""
"We are also not going to discuss data transfer improvements between LOPs and "
"clients or servers. Sure, there might be some easy and very effective "
"optimizations there (as we know that objects on LOPs are very likely "
"incompressible and not deltifying well), but this can be dealt with "
"separately in a separate effort."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:79
msgid ""
"In other words, the goal of this document is not to talk about all the "
"possible ways to optimize how Git could handle large blobs, but to describe "
"how a LOP based solution can already work well and alleviate a number of "
"current issues in the context of Git clients and servers sharing Git "
"objects."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:83
msgid ""
"Even if LOPs are used not very efficiently, they can still be useful and "
"worth using in some cases, as we will see in more details later in this "
"document:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:87
msgid ""
"they can make it simpler for clients to use promisor remotes and therefore "
"avoid fetching a lot of large blobs they might not need locally,"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:92
msgid ""
"they can make it significantly cheaper or easier for servers to host a "
"significant part of the current repository content, and even more to host "
"content with larger blobs or more large blobs than currently."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:94
#, no-wrap
msgid "I Issues with the current situation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:99
msgid ""
"Some statistics made on GitLab repos have shown that more than 75% of the "
"disk space is used by blobs that are larger than 1MB and often in a binary "
"format."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:103
msgid ""
"So even if users could use Git LFS or similar tools to store a lot of large "
"blobs out of their repos, it's a fact that in practice they don't do it as "
"much as they probably should."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:107
msgid ""
"On the server side ideally, the server should be able to decide for itself "
"how it stores things. It should not depend on users deciding to use tools "
"like Git LFS on some blobs or not."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:120
msgid ""
"It's much more expensive to store large blobs that don't delta compress well "
"on regular fast seeking drives (like SSDs) than on object storage (like "
"Amazon S3 or GCP Buckets). Using fast drives for regular Git repos makes "
"sense though, as serving regular Git content (blobs containing text or code) "
"needs drives where seeking is fast, but the content is relatively small. On "
"the other hand, object storage for Git LFS blobs makes sense as seeking "
"speed is not as important when dealing with large files, while costs are "
"more important. So the fact that users don't use Git LFS or similar tools "
"for a significant number of large blobs has likely some bad consequences on "
"the cost of repo storage for most Git hosting platforms."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:128
msgid ""
"Having large blobs handled in the same way as other blobs and Git objects in "
"Git repos instead of on object storage also has a cost in increased memory "
"and CPU usage, and therefore decreased performance, when creating "
"packfiles. (This is because Git tries to use delta compression or zlib "
"compression which is unlikely to work well on already compressed binary "
"content.) So it's not just a storage cost increase."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:133
msgid ""
"When a large blob has been committed into a repo, it might not be possible "
"to remove this blob from the repo without rewriting history, even if the "
"user then decides to use Git LFS or a similar tool to handle it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:136
msgid ""
"In fact Git LFS and similar tools are not very flexible in letting users "
"change their minds about the blobs they should handle or not."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:140
msgid ""
"Even when users are using Git LFS or similar tools, they are often "
"complaining that these tools require significant effort to set up, learn and "
"use correctly."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:142
#, no-wrap
msgid "II Main features of the \"Large Object Promisors\" solution"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:147
msgid ""
"The main features below should give a rough overview of how the solution may "
"work. Details about needed elements can be found in following sections."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:152
msgid ""
"Even if each feature below is very useful for the full solution, it is very "
"likely to be also useful on its own in some cases where the full solution is "
"not required. However, we'll focus primarily on the big picture here."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:158
msgid ""
"Also each feature doesn't need to be implemented entirely in Git "
"itself. Some could be scripts, hooks or helpers that are not part of the Git "
"repo. It would be helpful if those could be shared and improved on "
"collaboratively though. So we want to encourage sharing them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:160
msgid "Large blobs are stored on LOPs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:214
msgid ""
"LOPs can be implemented using object storage, like an Amazon S3 or GCP "
"Bucket or MinIO (which is open source under the GNU AGPLv3 license) to "
"actually store the large blobs, and can be accessed through a Git remote "
"helper (see linkgit:gitremote-helpers[7]) which makes the underlying object "
"storage appear like a remote to Git."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ^
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:216
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:329
#, no-wrap
msgid "Note"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:220
msgid ""
"A LOP can be a promisor remote accessed using a remote helper by both some "
"clients and the main remote."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ^
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:222
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:262
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:335
#, no-wrap
msgid "Rationale"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:226
msgid ""
"This looks like the simplest way to create LOPs that can cheaply handle many "
"large blobs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ^
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:228
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:271
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:340
#, no-wrap
msgid "Implementation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:233
msgid ""
"Remote helpers are quite easy to write as shell scripts, but it might be "
"more efficient and maintainable to write them using other languages like Go."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:235
msgid "Some already exist under open source licenses, for example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:237
msgid "https://github.com/awslabs/git-remote-s3"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:238
msgid "https://gitlab.com/eric.p.ju/git-remote-gs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:242
msgid ""
"Other ways to implement LOPs are certainly possible, but the goal of this "
"document is not to discuss how to best implement a LOP or its underlying "
"object storage (see the \"0) Non goals\" section above)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:244
msgid "LOP object storage can be Git LFS storage"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:260
msgid ""
"On the server side, a main remote should have a way to offload to a LOP all "
"its blobs with a size over a configurable threshold."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:269
msgid ""
"This makes it easy to set things up and to clean things up. For example, an "
"admin could use this to manually convert a repo not using LOPs to a repo "
"using a LOP. On a repo already using a LOP but where some users would "
"sometimes push large blobs, a cron job could use this to regularly make sure "
"the large blobs are moved to the LOP."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:277
msgid ""
"Using something based on `git repack --filter=...` to separate the blobs we "
"want to offload from the other Git objects could be a good idea. The missing "
"part is to connect to the LOP, check if the blobs we want to offload are "
"already there and if not send them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:279
msgid "A main remote should try to remain clean from large blobs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:327
msgid ""
"When a client clones from a main repo, there should be a protocol "
"negotiation so that the server can advertise one or more LOPs and so that "
"the client and the server can discuss if the client could directly use a LOP "
"the server is advertising. If the client and the server can agree on that, "
"then the client would be able to get the large blobs directly from the LOP "
"and the server would not need to fetch those blobs from the LOP to be able "
"to serve the client."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:333
msgid ""
"For fetches instead of clones, a protocol negotiation might not always "
"happen, see the \"What about fetches?\" FAQ entry below for details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:338
msgid "Security, configurability and efficiency of setting things up."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:345
msgid ""
"A \"promisor-remote\" protocol v2 capability looks like a good way to "
"implement this. The way the client and server use this capability could be "
"controlled by configuration variables."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:350
msgid ""
"Information that the server could send to the client through that protocol "
"could be things like: LOP name, LOP URL, filter-spec (for example "
"`blob:limit=<size>`) or just size limit that should be used as a filter when "
"cloning, token to be used with the LOP, etc."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:352
msgid "A client can offload to a LOP"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:418
msgid ""
"That could perhaps be useful in some cases, but for now it's more likely "
"that in most cases a single LOP will be advertised by the server and should "
"be used by the client."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:423
msgid ""
"A case where it could be useful for a server to advertise multiple LOPs is "
"if a LOP is better for some users while a different LOP is better for other "
"users. For example some clients might have a better connection to a LOP than "
"others."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:429
msgid ""
"In those cases it's the responsibility of the server to have some "
"documentation to help clients. It could say for example something like "
"\"Users in this part of the world might want to pick only LOP A as it is "
"likely to be better connected to them, while users in other parts of the "
"world should pick only LOP B for the same reason.\""
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ~
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:431
#, no-wrap
msgid "When should we trust or not trust the LOPs advertised by the server?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:438
msgid ""
"In some contexts, like in corporate setup where the server and all the "
"clients are parts of an internal network in a company where admins have all "
"the rights on every system, it's OK, and perhaps even a good thing, if the "
"clients fully trust the server, as it can help ensure that all the clients "
"are on the same page."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:447
msgid ""
"There are also contexts in which clients trust a code hosting platform "
"serving them some repos, but might not fully trust other users managing or "
"contributing to some of these repos. For example, the code hosting platform "
"could have hooks in place to check that any object it receives doesn't "
"contain malware or otherwise bad content. In this case it might be OK for "
"the client to use a main remote and its LOP if they are both hosted by the "
"code hosting platform, but not if the LOP is hosted elsewhere (where the "
"content is not checked)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:449
msgid "In other contexts, a client should just not trust a server."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:452
msgid ""
"So there should be different ways to configure how the client should behave "
"when a server advertises a LOP to it at clone time."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:456
msgid ""
"As the basic elements that a server can advertise about a LOP are a LOP name "
"and a LOP URL, the client should base its decision about accepting a LOP on "
"these elements."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:460
msgid ""
"One simple way to be very strict in the LOP it accepts is for example for "
"the client to check that the LOP is already configured on the client with "
"the same name and URL as what the server advertises."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:466
msgid ""
"In general default and \"safe\" settings should require that the LOP are "
"configured on the client separately from the \"promisor-remote\" protocol "
"and that the client accepts a LOP only when information about it from the "
"protocol matches what has been already configured separately."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ~
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:468
#, no-wrap
msgid "What about LOP names?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:473
msgid ""
"In some contexts, for example if the clients sometimes fetch from each "
"other, it can be a good idea for all the clients to use the same names for "
"all the remotes they use, including LOPs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:476
msgid ""
"In other contexts, each client might want to be able to give the name it "
"wants to each remote, including each LOP, it interacts with."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:479
msgid ""
"So there should be different ways to configure how the client accepts or not "
"the LOP name the server advertises."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:484
msgid ""
"If a default or \"safe\" setting is used, then as such a setting should "
"require that the LOP be configured separately, then the name would be "
"configured separately and there is no risk that the server could dictate a "
"name to a client."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ~
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:486
#, no-wrap
msgid "Could the main remote be bogged down by old or paranoid clients?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:492
msgid ""
"Yes, it could happen if there are too many clients that are either unwilling "
"to trust the main remote or that just don't implement the "
"\"promisor-remote\" protocol because they are too old or not fully "
"compatible with the 'git' client."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:498
msgid ""
"When serving such a client, the main remote has no other choice than to "
"first fetch from its LOP, to then be able to provide to the client "
"everything it requested. So the main remote, even if it has cleanup "
"mechanisms (see section II.4 above), would be burdened at least temporarily "
"with the large blobs it had to fetch from its LOP."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:507
msgid ""
"Not behaving like this would be breaking backward compatibility, and could "
"be seen as segregating clients. For example, it might be possible to "
"implement a special mode that allows the server to just reject clients that "
"don't implement the \"promisor-remote\" protocol or aren't willing to trust "
"the main remote. This mode might be useful in a special context like a "
"corporate environment. There is no plan to implement such a mode though, and "
"this should be discussed separately later anyway."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:513
msgid ""
"A better way to proceed is probably for the main remote to show a message "
"telling clients that don't implement the protocol or are unwilling to accept "
"the advertised LOP(s) that they would get faster clone and fetches by "
"upgrading client software or properly setting them up to accept LOP(s)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:520
msgid ""
"Waiting for clients to upgrade, monitoring these upgrades and limiting the "
"use of LOPs to repos that are not very frequently accessed might be other "
"good ways to make sure that some benefits are still reaped from LOPs. Over "
"time, as more and more clients upgrade and benefit from LOPs, using them in "
"more and more frequently accessed repos will become worth it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:524
msgid ""
"Corporate environments, where it might be easier to make sure that all the "
"clients are up-to-date and properly configured, could hopefully benefit more "
"and earlier from using LOPs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ~
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:526
#, no-wrap
msgid "What about fetches?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:534
msgid ""
"There are different kinds of fetches. A regular fetch happens when some refs "
"have been updated on the server and the client wants the ref updates and "
"possibly the new objects added with them. A \"backfill\" or \"lazy\" fetch, "
"on the contrary, happens when the client needs to use some objects it "
"already knows about but doesn't have because they are on a promisor remote."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ^
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:536
#, no-wrap
msgid "Regular fetch"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:545
msgid ""
"In a regular fetch, the client will contact the main remote and a protocol "
"negotiation will happen between them. It's a good thing that a protocol "
"negotiation happens every time, as the configuration on the client or the "
"main remote could have changed since the previous protocol negotiation. In "
"this case, the new protocol negotiation should ensure that the new fetch "
"will happen in a way that satisfies the new configuration of both the client "
"and the server."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:552
msgid ""
"In most cases though, the configurations on the client and the main remote "
"will not have changed between 2 fetches or between the initial clone and a "
"subsequent fetch. This means that the result of a new protocol negotiation "
"will be the same as the previous result, so the new fetch will happen in the "
"same way as the previous clone or fetch, using, or not using, the same "
"LOP(s) as last time."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ^
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:554
#, no-wrap
msgid "\"Backfill\" or \"lazy\" fetch"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:562
msgid ""
"When there is a backfill fetch, the client doesn't necessarily contact the "
"main remote first. It will try to fetch from its promisor remotes in the "
"order they appear in the config file, except that a remote configured using "
"the `extensions.partialClone` config variable will be tried last. See "
"link:partial-clone.html#using-many-promisor-remotes[the partial clone "
"documentation]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:565
msgid ""
"This is not new with this effort. In fact this is how multiple remotes have "
"already been working for around 5 years."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:569
msgid ""
"When using LOPs, having the main remote configured using "
"`extensions.partialClone`, so it's tried last, makes sense, as missing "
"objects should only be large blobs that are on LOPs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:573
msgid ""
"This means that a protocol negotiation will likely not happen as the missing "
"objects will be fetched from the LOPs, and then there will be nothing left "
"to fetch from the main remote."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:578
msgid ""
"To secure that, it could be a good idea for LOPs to require a token from the "
"client when it fetches from them. The client could get the token when "
"performing a protocol negotiation with the main remote (see section II.6 "
"above)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:580
#, no-wrap
msgid "V Future improvements"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:587
msgid ""
"It is expected that at the beginning using LOPs will be mostly worth it "
"either in a corporate context where the Git version that clients use can "
"easily be controlled, or on repos that are infrequently accessed. (See the "
"\"Could the main remote be bogged down by old or paranoid clients?\" section "
"in the FAQ above.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:591
msgid ""
"Over time, as more and more clients upgrade to a version that implements the "
"\"promisor-remote\" protocol v2 capability described above in section II.6), "
"it will be worth it to use LOPs more widely."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:595
msgid ""
"A lot of improvements may also help using LOPs more widely. Some of these "
"improvements are part of the scope of this document like the following:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:601
msgid ""
"Implementing a \"remote-object-info\" command in the `git cat-file --batch` "
"protocol and its variants to allow main remotes to respond to requests about "
"large blobs without fetching them. (Eric Ju has started working on this "
"based on previous work by Calvin Wan.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:604
msgid ""
"Creating better cleanup and offload mechanisms for main remotes and clients "
"to prevent accumulation of large blobs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:609
msgid ""
"Developing more sophisticated protocol negotiation capabilities between "
"clients and servers for handling LOPs, for example adding a filter-spec "
"(e.g., blob:limit=<size>) or size limit for filtering when cloning, or "
"adding a token for LOP authentication."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:612
msgid ""
"Improving security measures for LOP access, particularly around token "
"handling and authentication."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:618
msgid ""
"Developing standardized ways to configure and manage multiple LOPs across "
"different environments. Especially in the case where different LOPs serve "
"the same content to clients in different geographical locations, there is a "
"need for replication or synchronization between LOPs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:622
msgid ""
"Some improvements, including some that have been mentioned in the \"0)  Non "
"Goals\" section of this document, are out of the scope of this document:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:625
msgid "Implementing a new object representation for large blobs on the client side."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:629
msgid ""
"Developing pluggable ODBs or other object database backends that could chunk "
"large blobs, dedup the chunks and store them efficiently."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:632
msgid ""
"Optimizing data transfer between LOPs and clients/servers, particularly for "
"incompressible and non-deltifying content."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:637
msgid ""
"Creating improved client side tools for managing large objects more "
"effectively, for example tools for migrating from Git LFS or git-annex, or "
"tools to find which objects could be offloaded and how much disk space could "
"be reclaimed by offloading them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:641
msgid ""
"Some improvements could be seen as part of the scope of this document, but "
"might already have their own separate projects from the Git project, like:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:644
msgid ""
"Improving existing remote helpers to access object storage or developing new "
"ones."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:647
msgid "Improving existing object storage solutions or developing new ones."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:651
msgid ""
"Even though all the above improvements may help, this document and the LOP "
"effort should try to focus, at least first, on a relatively small number of "
"improvements mostly those that are in its current scope."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/technical/large-object-promisors.adoc:656
msgid ""
"For example introducing pluggable ODBs and a new object database backend is "
"likely a multi-year effort on its own that can happen separately in "
"parallel. It has different technical requirements, touches other part of the "
"Git code base and should have its own design document(s)."
msgstr ""
